 # Regularization and Loss Functions
-Also called: NeRF Loss Functions, Training Objectives
+
 
 ## Overview
-This family encompasses the mathematical terms added to the total loss function during Neural Radiance Field (NeRF) optimization to constrain the solution space, improve geometric accuracy, and mitigate artifacts such as floaters or over-saturation [S1, 8727-depth-and-geometry-supervision.md]. It solves the ill-posed nature of inverse volume rendering, where photometric consistency alone often leads to ambiguous geometries or poor generalization in sparse-view settings [S1, 8727-depth-and-geometry-supervision.md]. What sets this family apart from its siblings, such as [Training Ray Marching and Integration](2199-training-ray-marching-and-integration.md) (which handles the forward rendering process) and [NeRF Training Loop](970-nerf-training-loop.md) (which manages the optimization schedule), is the specific definition of the error metrics and priors applied to the density $\sigma$ and color $\mathbf{c}$ fields [S1, 8727-depth-and-geometry-supervision.md].
+Regularization and Loss Functions define the objective criteria used to optimize Neural Radiance Fields (NeRFs) beyond simple photometric reconstruction. They solve the under-constrained nature of volume rendering, particularly in sparse-view settings, by preventing artifacts such as "floaters," enforcing geometric smoothness, and aligning semantic content. This family is distinguished from [Training Ray Marching and Integration](2199-training-ray-marching-and-integration.md) by focusing on the *what* (the loss terms) rather than the *how* (the integration method), and from [Training Configuration](2332-training-configuration.md) by providing the mathematical definitions of the optimization targets.
 
 ## Shared principles
-All methods in this family share the fundamental structure of an augmented loss function. The total loss $\mathcal{L}_{\text{total}}$ is a weighted sum of the standard photometric reconstruction loss and one or more regularization terms $\mathcal{L}_k$:
+All methods in this family operate within an augmented loss structure where the total loss $\mathcal{L}_{\text{total}}$ is a weighted sum of the standard photometric reconstruction loss and one or more regularization terms $\mathcal{L}_k$ [S1, 7475-geometric-baseline-regularization.md, 8582-regnerf.md]:
 
 $$ \mathcal{L}_{\text{total}} = \mathcal{L}_{\text{photo}} + \sum_k \lambda_k \mathcal{L}_k $$
